net/sched: sch_cake: add hysteresis to the cake_mq shared-shaper count

cake_mq divides one shared rate cap among the TX queues that are
currently active:

	new_rate = cfg->rate_bps / num_active_qs

num_active_qs is recomputed by whichever queue wins the sync tick, by
sampling every queue's instantaneous backlog (other_qlen) and its last
activity stamp. That sample is taken every g_sync_time_ns, 200us, and
the resulting value is published as-is.

Measured on a GL-MT6000 (16 TX queues on the WAN, shared cap 900Mbit),
with the same bounded single-stream upload run twice two minutes apart,
on the same image, toggling only the activity window through debugfs:

  window 0    (backlog alone)   the 16 children read 1 to 4 at once
  window 1ms  (backlog or       the 16 children read 1 to 5, then up to
               active <1ms)     12 over the tail of the run

The spread persists in both arms, so it is not the window that is
unstable: it is the sampling. Two consecutive ticks can count very
different numbers of queues, and each child caches whichever sample it
drew, so the 16 children apply different divisors at the same instant.

The per-queue packet deltas over the run also show the flow does not
cover the whole device: five of the sixteen queues carried fewer than
110 packets while the busiest carried about 168000. Both arms therefore
under-count the queues that actually compete, and neither is stable.

Stop the count from tracking the sampling noise. It now rises
immediately -- so it never under-estimates concurrency, which is what
would let the aggregate exceed the shared cap -- and falls by at most
g_active_release queues per tick. At the 200us tick a release of 1
brings a count of 16 back to 1 in about 3ms, well inside the timescale
of a flow starting or stopping.

The release is exposed through debugfs, like sync_time_ns and
active_window_ns, so it can be retuned without reflashing. Setting it
to 0 disables the hysteresis and publishes the instantaneous sample
again, which gives the control arm of a live A/B.


--- a/net/sched/sch_cake.c
+++ b/net/sched/sch_cake.c
@@ -94,6 +94,13 @@
  * ce bruit et plafonneraient un flux unique au tiers de la ligne.
  */
 static u64 g_active_window_ns = 1*NSEC_PER_MSEC;
+/* Hysteresis du compte partage : nombre de files dont la descente
+ * peut etre absorbee en un tick de synchronisation. Le compte monte
+ * immediatement (il ne sous-estime donc jamais la concurrence, ce qui
+ * tiendrait le cap agrege) et ne redescend que de cette valeur par
+ * tick, au lieu de recopier le bruit d'echantillonnage du backlog.
+ */
+static u32 g_active_release = 1;
 static unsigned int cake_mq_queue_count(const struct net_device *dev)
 {
 	unsigned int ntx = READ_ONCE(dev->real_num_tx_queues);
@@ -2083,6 +2090,18 @@
 						num_active_qs++;
 				}
 
+				/* Hysteresis : montee immediate, descente d'au plus
+				 * g_active_release par tick. active_queues porte ici la
+				 * derniere valeur publiee. g_active_release = 0 desactive
+				 * l'hysteresis (valeur instantanee), ce qui donne le bras
+				 * temoin d'un A/B live.
+				 */
+				if (g_active_release && num_active_qs < active_queues) {
+					u32 drop = active_queues - num_active_qs;
+
+					num_active_qs = active_queues - min(drop, g_active_release);
+				}
+
 				WRITE_ONCE(cfg->shared_last_checked, now);
 				WRITE_ONCE(cfg->shared_active_queues, num_active_qs);
 				active_queues = num_active_qs;
@@ -3425,6 +3444,8 @@
 		debugfs_create_u64("sync_time_ns", 0644, cake_mq_debugfs, &g_sync_time_ns);
 		debugfs_create_u64("active_window_ns", 0644, cake_mq_debugfs,
 				   &g_active_window_ns);
+		debugfs_create_u32("active_release", 0644, cake_mq_debugfs,
+				   &g_active_release);
 	}
 
 	return ret;
